WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.510
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:10.470
This work presents a learning-based method
that is able to take just a handful of photos,

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:14.760
and use those to synthesize a moving virtual
character.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:20.150
Not only that, but it can also synthesize
these faces from new viewpoints that the AI

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:22.019
hasn’t seen before.

00:00:22.019 --> 00:00:27.550
These results are truly sublime, however,
hold on to your papers, because it also works

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:31.589
from as little as just one input image.

00:00:31.589 --> 00:00:33.920
This we refer to as 1-shot learning.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:40.280
You see some examples here, but wait a second…really,
just one image?

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.640
If all it needs is really just one photo,
this means that we can use famous photographs,

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:49.750
and even paintings, and synthesize animations
for them.

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:51.570
Look at that!

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:57.199
Of course, if we show multiple photos to the
AI, it is able to synthesize better output

00:00:57.199 --> 00:01:02.660
results, you see such a progression here as
a function of the amount of input data.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:07.630
The painting part I find to be particularly
cool because it strays away from the kind

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:13.530
of data the neural networks were trained on,
which is photos, however, if we have proper

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:19.530
intelligence, the AI can learn how different
parts of the human face move, and generalize

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.110
this knowledge to paintings as well.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:27.050
The underlying laws are the same, only the
style of the output is different.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:28.780
Absolutely amazing.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:34.880
The paper also showcases an extensive comparison
section to previous works, and, as you see

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:38.750
here, nothing really compares to this kind
of quality.

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:43.630
I have heard the quote “any sufficiently
advanced technology is indistinguishable from

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:50.670
magic” so many times in my life, and I was
like, OK, well, maybe, but I’m telling you

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:55.680
- this is one of those times when I really
felt that I am seeing magic at work on my

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:56.900
computer screen.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:02.140
So, I know what you’re thinking - how can
all this wizardry be done?

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:07.650
This paper proposes a novel architecture where
3 neural networks work together.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:14.000
One, the Embedder takes colored images with
landmark information and compresses it down

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.440
into the essence of these images, two, the
Generator takes a set of landmarks, a crude

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:26.280
approximation of the human face, and synthesizes
a photorealistic result from it.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:32.220
And three, the Discriminator looks at both
real and fake images and tries to learn how

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:33.950
to tell them apart.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:40.170
As a result, these networks learn together,
and over time, they improve together, so much

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:45.840
so that they can create these amazing animations
from just one source photo.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:51.040
The authors also released a statement on the
purpose and effects of this technology, which

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.730
I’ll leave here for a few seconds for our
interested viewers.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:59.310
This work was partly done at the Samsung AI
lab and Skoltech.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.650
Congratulations to both institutions.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.110
Killer paper.

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:05.760
Make sure to check it out in the video description.

